{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JDEx2e2G8pjdP6Q2goosbh","type":"tool_result","content":"# NodePort Services and Virtual IPs - Information Not Found\n\nBased on my review of the provided web page content, I cannot find any information about:\n\n- The default node port range\n- The `--service-node-port-range` flag\n- Port allocation for NodePort services\n- How kube-proxy handles NodePort\n\nAdditionally, while the page discusses the virtual IP mechanism for Services in general, I could not locate a specific statement confirming that \"the cluster IP is virtual / nothing listening on it\" in the extracted content provided.\n\nThe page does mention that:\n- \"The `kube-proxy` component is responsible for implementing a _virtual IP_ mechanism for [Services](/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/) of `type` other than [`ExternalName`]\"\n- kube-proxy \"configures the node to capture traffic to the Service's `clusterIP` and `port`, and redirect that traffic to one of the Service's endpoints\"\n\nHowever, the specific details you're asking about regarding NodePort configuration and the virtual nature of cluster IPs are not present in this particular section of the documentation that was extracted."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bf4a748b-2e29-454c-ab94-00025388e7de","uuid":"010cfda2-ef38-4124-8630-ea5f3100c6e7","timestamp":"2026-08-25T00:53:59.761Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":542652,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# NodePort Services and Virtual IPs - Information Not Found\n\nBased on my review of the provided web page content, I cannot find any information about:\n\n- The default node port range\n- The `--service-node-port-range` flag\n- Port allocation for NodePort services\n- How kube-proxy handles NodePort\n\nAdditionally, while the page discusses the virtual IP mechanism for Services in general, I could not locate a specific statement confirming that \"the cluster IP is virtual / nothing listening on it\" in the extracted content provided.\n\nThe page does mention that:\n- \"The `kube-proxy` component is responsible for implementing a _virtual IP_ mechanism for [Services](/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/) of `type` other than [`ExternalName`]\"\n- kube-proxy \"configures the node to capture traffic to the Service's `clusterIP` and `port`, and redirect that traffic to one of the Service's endpoints\"\n\nHowever, the specific details you're asking about regarding NodePort configuration and the virtual nature of cluster IPs are not present in this particular section of the documentation that was extracted.","durationMs":4414,"url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/networking/virtual-ips/#nodeport-services"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AFsTyarUHeMLKnmvFuyAKY","type":"tool_result","content":"# type: NodePort Section\n\nHere is the verbatim extraction of the \"type: NodePort\" section:\n\nIf you set the `type` field to `NodePort`, the Kubernetes control plane allocates a port from a range specified by `--service-node-port-range` flag (default: 30000-32767). Each node proxies that port (the same port number on every node) into your Service. Your Service reports the allocated node port in its `.spec.nodePort` field.\n\nIf you want a specific port number, you can specify a value in the `nodePort` field. Kubernetes will allocate that port or report back that the API transaction failed if the port is already in use. This means you need to care about possible port collisions yourself.\n\nIf you didn't specify a `nodePort`, one will be allocated for you.\n\nIf you set the `nodePort` value, it must be within the `--service-node-port-range` range configured for that cluster.\n\nUsing a NodePort gives you the freedom to set up your own load balancing solution, to configure environments that are not fully supported by Kubernetes, or even to expose one or more nodes' IPs directly.\n\nNote that Service of type NodePort and type LoadBalancer are supersets of type ClusterIP. That is, a NodePort Service is reachable through the cluster IP as well.\n\n**The three port fields and how they differ:**\n\n- `nodePort` - the port exposed on each node\n- `port` - the port exposed by the service\n- `targetPort` - the port on the pod that the traffic is directed to\n\nKubernetes also allocates a cluster IP for a NodePort Service (as stated in the final note that NodePort Services are \"supersets of type ClusterIP\")."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bf4a748b-2e29-454c-ab94-00025388e7de","uuid":"23ec32f0-2592-4a2d-bc94-3dd5274c1fb1","timestamp":"2026-08-25T00:53:59.978Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":574894,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# type: NodePort Section\n\nHere is the verbatim extraction of the \"type: NodePort\" section:\n\nIf you set the `type` field to `NodePort`, the Kubernetes control plane allocates a port from a range specified by `--service-node-port-range` flag (default: 30000-32767). Each node proxies that port (the same port number on every node) into your Service. Your Service reports the allocated node port in its `.spec.nodePort` field.\n\nIf you want a specific port number, you can specify a value in the `nodePort` field. Kubernetes will allocate that port or report back that the API transaction failed if the port is already in use. This means you need to care about possible port collisions yourself.\n\nIf you didn't specify a `nodePort`, one will be allocated for you.\n\nIf you set the `nodePort` value, it must be within the `--service-node-port-range` range configured for that cluster.\n\nUsing a NodePort gives you the freedom to set up your own load balancing solution, to configure environments that are not fully supported by Kubernetes, or even to expose one or more nodes' IPs directly.\n\nNote that Service of type NodePort and type LoadBalancer are supersets of type ClusterIP. That is, a NodePort Service is reachable through the cluster IP as well.\n\n**The three port fields and how they differ:**\n\n- `nodePort` - the port exposed on each node\n- `port` - the port exposed by the service\n- `targetPort` - the port on the pod that the traffic is directed to\n\nKubernetes also allocates a cluster IP for a NodePort Service (as stated in the final note that NodePort Services are \"supersets of type ClusterIP\").","durationMs":6019,"url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/#type-nodeport"}}
