{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ends efficiently.\n\nEndpointSlices track the IP addresses of backend endpoints. EndpointSlices are normally associated with a Service and the backend endpoints typically represent Pods.\n\n## EndpointSlice API\n\n{{< feature-state for"}},"session_id":"bf4a748b-2e29-454c-ab94-00025388e7de","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"2e6fa77c-42d2-456d-a820-e4554a1f95c4"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"_k8s_version=\"v1.21\" state=\"stable\" >}}\n\nIn Kubernetes, an EndpointSlice contains references to a set of network endpoints. The control plane automatically creates EndpointSlices for any Kubernetes Service that has a selector specified. These"}},"session_id":"bf4a748b-2e29-454c-ab94-00025388e7de","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"cc0bae77-86c1-4798-88ef-d5ee37ae1cce"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" EndpointSlices include references to all the Pods that match the Service selector.\n\nEndpointSlices act as the source of truth for kube-proxy when it comes to how to route internal traffic.\n\n### Address types\n\nEndpointSlices support two address types:\n\n* I"}},"session_id":"bf4a748b-2e29-454c-ab94-00025388e7de","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"35dba3d5-7387-4dad-afdf-c1aef8932b5c"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"Pv4\n* IPv6\n\nEach `EndpointSlice` object represents a specific IP address type.\n\n[Dual-stack discussion beyond this list trimmed as out of scope — see § Open questions #9,\nwhich records dual-stack as a de"}},"session_id":"bf4a748b-2e29-454c-ab94-00025388e7de","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"211d98b6-4151-4bbe-93d7-c41e0fe98967"}
